---
name: art-consistency
description: What actually holds AI-generated art together across hundreds of assets in {{PROJECT_NAME}}
metadata:
  type: project
---

Image models do not remember previous calls. Two assets generated from equally
good prompts a week apart will not match. Three mechanisms, in order of how much
they contribute, keep {{PROJECT_NAME}} looking like one game:

1. **The frozen reference sheet.** Every generation is conditioned on the same
   locked images. This does most of the work; a prompt-spec with an empty
   `reference_images` list turns a conditioned generation into an independent
   roll.
2. **The style profile's machine rules.** Palette distance, colour count, contour
   width, alpha hygiene. Blunt, but they reject the obviously-off before anyone
   spends attention on it.
3. **The prompt skeleton.** Wording that has been observed to hold the style,
   reused verbatim rather than paraphrased.

**How to apply:** never generate production art before STYLE LOCK, never leave
`reference_images` empty, and when an asset comes back wrong, fix the prompt-spec
rather than the pixels. Three attempts maximum — past that, the spec or the
profile is wrong, and more attempts only cost money.
